-- =====================================================================
-- A daily margin call run, governed for completeness.
--
-- The story, for a reader who has never traded a thing.
--
-- When you trade on credit - futures, cleared swaps, an energy forward -
-- you do not pay the whole value up front. Instead you keep a deposit
-- with your broker or clearing house, called margin, as a cushion against
-- the position moving against you. Each day the position is re-priced,
-- and your account's equity (the deposit plus or minus what you have made
-- or lost) goes up and down with the market.
--
-- Two levels matter. The REQUIRED margin is the level your account is
-- supposed to sit at. The MAINTENANCE margin is a lower floor. As long as
-- your equity stays above the floor, nobody bothers you. The moment it
-- drops below the floor, the firm issues a MARGIN CALL: a demand that you
-- top your account back up to the required level by the next morning. The
-- amount of the call is exactly the gap - what you need to add to get from
-- where you are back up to where you should be.
--
-- Every day a risk engine sweeps the whole book, works out which accounts
-- have fallen through the floor, and produces a batch of calls. That batch
-- is the thing this model governs.
--
-- Why is that worth governing? Because the dangerous mistake is not a
-- wrong call - it is a MISSING one. If the engine quietly leaves an
-- under-margined account out of the run, that account is now carrying a
-- losing position with too little cushion, and if it defaults the firm
-- eats the loss. Whole firms have failed this way in volatile markets.
-- So the control we want is not just "every call is correct" but "every
-- account that should be called IS called" - completeness, not merely
-- correctness. A rule that only forbids bad calls would happily wave
-- through a run that forgot half the book.
--
-- The risk engine is ordinary software - it might have a bug, it might be
-- fed stale prices, it might be a model nobody fully understands. We do
-- not have to trust it. It PROPOSES the whole batch as one all-or-nothing
-- decision, and the runtime admits the run only if it is complete and
-- exact. If a single under-margined account is missing, the entire run is
-- refused; nothing is recorded until the batch is right.
--
-- A note on words. The runtime never stores "facts". It stores CLAIMS -
-- statements the firm has accepted and is prepared to stand behind ("this
-- account's equity is 80,000 dollars", "this account was called for
-- 30,000 in today's run"). An INVARIANT below is a rule the runtime will
-- not let any change break. A `require` is a gate on a single action: a
-- yes/no question asked before that action is allowed to proceed.
-- =====================================================================


program margin_call_run


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The book, as it stands at the start of the run.
--
-- These three claims describe each account before today's sweep. In a
-- real deployment they are kept current by their own governed processes
-- (positions are marked to market, deposits are posted and withdrawn);
-- here they are simply the state the run reads. Money is written with its
-- unit, `Decimal[USD]`, so the runtime will not let dollars be compared
-- with or added to anything that is not also dollars.
--
-- `unique by (account)` says the keys in brackets determine the whole
-- claim: an account has exactly one required margin, one maintenance
-- floor, one equity figure. The runtime refuses a second, contradicting
-- one - you cannot quietly give an account two different equities.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

predicate RequiredMargin(account: Subject, level: Decimal[USD])
  unique by (account)

predicate MaintenanceMargin(account: Subject, floor: Decimal[USD])
  unique by (account)

predicate AccountEquity(account: Subject, equity: Decimal[USD])
  unique by (account)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What the run records.
--
-- `MarginRun` is the run's header - which run, as of which date. A
-- `MarginCall` is one demand: this run called this account for this
-- amount. `unique by (run, account)` is the control against a double
-- call - one account cannot be called twice in the same run, however the
-- engine phrases its batch.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

predicate MarginRun(run: Subject, as_of: Date)
  unique by (run)

predicate MarginCall(run: Subject, account: Subject, amount: Decimal[USD])
  unique by (run, account)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The message that leaves the building.
--
-- An `intent` is something the runtime will hand to the outside world
-- once the run commits - here, the notice sent to the account holder
-- demanding the top-up. It is emitted inside the transformation but
-- delivered after the commit, so a refused run sends nothing.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

intent MarginCallIssued(run: Subject, account: Subject, amount: Decimal[USD])


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The correctness rule: a call is exactly the top-up.
--
-- An invariant holds over all admitted state, forever. This one says:
-- wherever a margin call exists, its amount is precisely the account's
-- required level minus its current equity - the gap that brings the
-- account back to where it should be. A call for a made-up number cannot
-- be admitted, because committing it would break this rule.
--
-- Note what this does NOT say. It does not, by itself, demand that any
-- account be called. Forbidding wrong amounts is not the same as
-- requiring the right accounts to appear - that second, harder half is
-- the completeness gate inside the run below.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

invariant call_amount_is_the_top_up:
  MarginCall(run, account, amount)
  implies (RequiredMargin(account, required)
           and AccountEquity(account, equity)
           and amount = required - equity)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The run itself.
--
-- The risk engine hands the runtime three things: an id for the run, the
-- date it is as of, and `called_accounts` - the WHOLE LIST of accounts it
-- is calling, submitted as one value. This is the heart of the example:
-- a batch of unknown size, proposed and judged as a single all-or-nothing
-- decision, rather than dribbled in one account at a time.
--
-- Read the body top to bottom.
--
-- First the completeness gate. `require` asks a yes/no question and lets
-- the run proceed only on a yes. The question here is the whole point of
-- the model: is there any account that has fallen below its maintenance
-- floor and is NOT in the batch? If such an account exists, the answer is
-- no and the entire run is refused. `acct in called_accounts` tests
-- membership of the proposed list, so this gate compares the batch the
-- engine submitted against the accounts the book says must be called. A
-- forgotten account is caught here, before anything is recorded.
--
-- Then the work. For each account in the batch, the runtime reads its
-- required level, its equity, and its floor (`bind` looks up the single
-- matching claim and names its fields). The second `require` guards
-- against the opposite mistake - calling an account that is fine: only an
-- account actually below its floor may be called. The call amount is the
-- gap, and a `MarginCall` is recorded and a notice emitted.
--
-- So the run commits only if the batch is complete (every under-margined
-- account present, by the gate), exact (no adequately-margined account
-- called, by the per-account require; no wrong amount, by the invariant),
-- and free of double calls (by `unique by (run, account)`). Miss one
-- account and the whole run is refused - which is exactly the protection
-- a margin process is supposed to give.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

transformation issue_margin_run(run_id, as_of_date, called_accounts):
  admit MarginRun(run_id, as_of_date)

  require not (exists acct:
    AccountEquity(acct, equity)
    and MaintenanceMargin(acct, floor)
    and equity < floor
    and not (acct in called_accounts))

  for acct in called_accounts:
    bind RequiredMargin(acct, required)
    bind AccountEquity(acct, equity)
    bind MaintenanceMargin(acct, floor)
    require equity < floor
    let call_amount = required - equity
    admit MarginCall(run_id, acct, call_amount)
    emit MarginCallIssued(run_id, acct, call_amount)
